9 |     a : array_like\n3290 |         Array containing numbers whose mean is desired. If `a` is not an\n3291 |         array, a conversion is attempted.\n3292 |     axis : None or int or tuple of ints, optional\n3293 |         Axis or axes along which the means are computed. The default is to\n3294 |         compute the mean of the flattened array.\n3295 | \n3296 |         .. versionadded:: 1.7.0\n3297 | \n3298 |         If this is a tuple of ints, a mean is performed over multiple axes,\n3299 |         instead of a single axis or all the axes as before.\n3300 |     dtype : data-type, optional\n3301 |         Type to use in computing the mean.  For integer inputs, the default\n3302 |         is `float64`; for floating point inputs, it is the same as the\n3303 |         input dtype.\n3304 |     out : ndarray, optional\n3305 |         Alternate output array in which to place the result.  The default\n3306 |         is ``None``; if provided, it must have the same shape as the\n3307 |         expected output, but the type will be cast if necessary.\n3308 |         See :ref:`ufuncs-output-type` for more details.\n3309 | \n3310 |     keepdims : bool, optional\n3311 |         If this is set to True, the axes which are reduced are left\n3312 |         in the result as dimensions with size one. With this option,\n3313 |         the result will broadcast correctly against the input array.\n3314 | \n3315 |         If the default value is passed, then `keepdims` will not be\n3316 |         passed through to the `mean` method of sub-classes of\n3317 |         `ndarray`, however any non-default value will be.  If the\n3318 |         sub-class' method does not implement `keepdims` any\n3319 |         exceptions will be raised.\n3320 | \n3321 |     Returns\n3322 |     -------\n3323 |     m : ndarray, see dtype parameter above\n3324 |         If `out=None`, returns a new array containing the mean values,\n3325 |         otherwise a reference to the output array is returned.\n3326 |