   exact version is highly recommended.\n704 | \n705 |     data_id : int, default=None\n706 |         OpenML ID of the dataset. The most specific way of retrieving a\n707 |         dataset. If data_id is not given, name (and potential version) are\n708 |         used to obtain a dataset.\n709 | \n710 |     data_home : str, default=None\n711 |         Specify another download and cache folder for the data sets. By default\n712 |         all scikit-learn data is stored in '~/scikit_learn_data' subfolders.\n713 | \n714 |     target_column : str, list or None, default='default-target'\n715 |         Specify the column name in the data to use as target. If\n716 |         'default-target', the standard target column a stored on the server\n717 |         is used. If ``None``, all columns are returned as data and the\n718 |         target is ``None``. If list (of strings), all columns with these names\n719 |         are returned as multi-target (Note: not all scikit-learn classifiers\n720 |         can handle all types of multi-output combinations)\n721 | \n722 |     cache : bool, default=True\n723 |         Whether to cache downloaded datasets using joblib.\n724 | \n725 |     return_X_y : bool, default=False\n726 |         If True, returns ``(data, target)`` instead of a Bunch object. See\n727 |         below for more information about the `data` and `target` objects.\n728 | \n729 |     as_frame : bool or 'auto', default=False\n730 |         If True, the data is a pandas DataFrame including columns with\n731 |         appropriate dtypes (numeric, string or categorical). The target is\n732 |         a pandas DataFrame or Series depending on the number of target_columns.\n733 |         The Bunch will contain a ``frame`` attribute with the target and the\n734 |         data. If ``return_X_y`` is True, then ``(data, target)`` will be pandas\n735 |         DataFrames or Series as describe above.\n736 |         If as_frame is 'auto', the data and target will be converted to\n737 |         DataFrame or Series as if as_frame is set to True, unless the dataset\n738 |         is stored in sparse format.\n739 | \n740 |     Returns\n741 |     -------\n742 | \n743 |     data : :class:`~sklearn.utils.Bunch`\n744 |         Dictionary-like object, with the following attributes.\n745 | \n746 |         data : np.array, scipy.sparse.csr_matrix of floats, or pandas DataFrame\n747 |             The feature matrix. Categorical features are encoded as ordinals.\n748 |         target : np.array, pandas Series or DataFrame\n749 |             The regression target or classification labels, if applicable.\n750 |             Dtype is float if numeric, and object if categorical. If\n751 |             ``as_frame`` is True, ``target`` is a pandas object.\n752 |         DESCR : str\n753 |             The full description of the dataset\n754 |         feature_names : list\n755 |             The names of the dataset columns\n756 |         target_names: list\n757 |             The names of the target columns\n758 | \n759 |         .. versionadded:: 0.22\n760 | \n761 |         categories : dict or None\n762 |             Maps each categorical feature name to a list of values, such\n763 |             that the value encoded as i is ith in the list. If ``as_frame``\n764 |             is True, this is None.\n765 |         details : dict\n766 |             More metadata from OpenML\n767 |         frame : pandas DataFrame\n768 |             Only present when `as_frame=True`. DataFrame with ``data`` and\n769 |             ``target``.\n770 | \n771 |     (data, target) : tuple if ``return_X_y`` is True\n772 | \n773 |         .. note:: EXPERIMENTAL\n774 | \n775 |             This interface is **experimental** and subsequent releases may\n776 |             change attributes without notice (although there should only be\n777 |             minor changes to ``data`` and ``target``).\n778 | \n779 |         Missing values in the 'data' are represented as NaN's. Missing values\n780 |         in 'target' are represented as NaN's (numerical target) or None\n781 |         (categorical target)\n782 |     \"\"\"\n783 |     data_home = get_data_home(data_home=data_home)\n784 |     data_home = join(data_home, 'openml')\n785 |     if cache is False:\n786 |         # no caching will be applied\n787 |         data_home = None\n788 | \n789 |     # check valid function arguments. data_id XOR (name, version) should be\n790 |     # provided\n791 |     if name is not None:\n792 |         # OpenML is case-insensitive, but the caching mechanism is not\n793 |         # convert all data names (str) to lower case\n794 |         name = name.lower()\n795 |         if data_id is not None:\n796 |             raise ValueError(\n797 |                 \"Dataset data_id={} and name={} passed, but you can only \"\n798 |                 \"specify a numeric data_id or a name, not \"\n799 |                 \"both.\".format(data_id, name))\n800 |         data_info = _get_data_info_by_name(name, version, data_home)\n801 |         data_id = data_info['did']\n802 |     elif data_id is not None:\n803 |         # from the previous if statement, it is given that name is None\n804 |         if version != \"active\":\n805 |             raise ValueError(\n806 |                 \"Dataset data_id={} and version={} passed, but you can only \"\n807 |                 \"specify a numeric data_id or a version, not \"\n808 |                 \"both.\".format(data_id, name))\n809 |     else:\n810 |         raise ValueError(\n811 |             \"Neither name nor data_id are provided. Please provide name or \"\n812 |             \"data_id.\")\n813 | \n814 |     data_description = _get_data_description_by_id(data_id, data_home)\n815 |     if data_description['status'] != \"active\":\n816 |         warn(\"Version {} of dataset {} is inactive, meaning that issues have \"\n817 |              \"been found in the dataset. Try using a newer version from \"\n818 |              \"this URL: {}\".format(\n819 |                 data_description['version'],\n820 |                 data_description['name'],\n821 |                 data_description['url']))\n822 |     if 'error' in data_description:\n823 |         warn(\"OpenML registered a problem with the dataset. It might be \"\n824 |              \"unusable. Error: {}\".format(data_description['error']))\n825 |     if 'warning' in data_description:\n826 |         warn(\"OpenML raised a warning on the dataset. It might be \"\n827 |              \"unusable. Warning: {}\".format(data_description['warning']))\n828 | \n829 |     return_sparse = False\n830 |     if data_description['format'].lower() == 'sparse_arff':\n831 |         return_sparse = True\n832 | \n833 |     if as_frame == 'auto':\n834 |         as_frame = not return_sparse\n835 | \n836 |     if as_frame and return_sparse:\n837 |         raise ValueError('Cannot return dataframe with sparse data')\n838 | \n839 |     # download data features, meta-info about column types\n840 |     features_list = _get_data_features(data_id, data_home)\n841 | \n842 |     if not as_frame:\n843 |         for feature in features_list:\n844 |             if 'true' in (feature['is_ignore'], feature['is_row_identifier']):\n845 |                 continue\n846 |             if feature['data_type'] == 'string':\n847 |                 raise ValueError('STRING attributes are not supported for '\n848 |                                  'array representation. Try as_frame=True')\n849 | \n850 |     if target_column == \"default-target\":\n851 |         # determines the default target based on the data feature results\n852 |         # (which is currently more reliable than the data description;\n853 |         # see issue: https://github.com/openml/OpenML/issues/768)\n854 |         target_columns = [feature['name'] for feature in features_list\n855 |                           if feature['is_target'] == 'true']\n856 |     elif isinstance(target_column, str):\n857 |         # for code-simplicity, make target_column by default a list\n858 |         target_columns = [target_column]\n859 |     elif target_column is None:\n860 |         target_columns = []\n861 |     elif isinstance(target_column, list):\n862 |         target_columns = target_column\n863 |     else:\n864 |         raise TypeError(\"Did not recognize type of target_column\"\n865 |                         \"Should be str, list or None. Got: \"\n866 |                         \"{}\".format(type(target_column)))\n867 |     data_columns = _valid_data_column_names(features_list,\n868 |                                             target_columns)\n869 | \n870 |     shape: Optional[Tuple[int, int]]\n871 |     # determine arff encoding to return\n872 |     if not return_sparse:\n873 |         # The shape must include the ignored features to keep the right indexes\n874 |         # during the arff data conversion.\n875 |         data_qualities = _get_data_qualities(data_id, data_home)\n876 |         shape = _get_num_samples(data_qualities), len(features_list)\n877 |     else:\n878 |         shape = None\n879 | \n880 |     # obtain the data\n881 |     url = _DATA_FILE.format(data_description['file_id'])\n882 |     bunch = _download_data_to_bunch(url, return_sparse, data_home,\n883 |                                     as_frame=as_frame,\n884 |                                     features_list=features_list, shape=shape,\n885 |                                     target_columns=target_columns,\n886 |                                     data_columns=data_columns)\n887 | \n888 |     if return_X_y:\n889 |         return bunch.data, bunch.target\n890 | \n891 |     description = \"{}\\n\\nDownloaded from openml.org.\".format(\n892 |         data_description.pop('description'))\n893 | \n894 |     bunch.update(\n895 |         DESCR=description, details=data_description,\n896 |         url=\"https://www.openml.org/d/{}\".format(data_id))\n897 | \n898 |     return bunch\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_rcv1.py:fetch_rcv1`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 80 | def fetch_rcv1(*, data_home=None, subset='all', download_if_missing=True,\n 81 |                random_state=None, shuffle=False, return_X_y=False):\n 82 |     \"\"\"Load the RCV1 multilabel dataset (classification).\n 83 | \n 84 |     Download it if necessary.\n 85 | \n 86 |     Version: RCV1-v2, vectors, full sets, topics multilabels.\n 87 | \n 88 |     =================   =====================\n 89 |     Classes                               103\n 90 |     Samples total                      804414\n 91 |     Dimensionality                      47236\n 92 |     Features            real, between 0 and 1\n 93 |     =================   =====================\n 94 | \n 95 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <rcv1_dataset>`.\n 96 | \n 97 |     .. versionadded:: 0.17\n 98 | \n 99 |     Parameters\n100 |     ----------\n101 |     data_home : str, default=None\n102 |         Specify another download and cache folder for the datasets. By default\n103 |         all scikit-learn data is stored in '~/scikit_learn_data' subfolders.\n104 | \n105 |     subset : {'train', 'test', 'all'}, default='all'\n106 |         Select the dataset to load: 'train' for the training set\n107 |         (23149 samples), 'test' for the test set (781265 samples),\n108 |         'all' for both, with the training samples first if shuffle is False.\n109 |         This follows the official LYRL2004 chronological split.\n110 | \n111 |     download_if_missing : bool, default=True\n112 |         If False, raise a IOError if the data is not locally available\n113 |         instead of trying to download the data from the source site.\n114 | \n115 |     random_state : int or RandomState instance, default=None\n116 |         Determines random number generation for dataset shuffling. Pass an int\n117 |         for reproducible output across multiple function calls.\n118 |         See :term:`Glossary <random_state>`.\n119 | \n120 |     shuffle : bool, default=False\n121 |         Whether to shuffle dataset.\n122 | \n123 |     return_X_y : bool, default=False\n124 |         If True, returns ``(dataset.data, dataset.target)`` instead of a Bunch\n125 |         object. See below for more information about the `dataset.data` and\n126 |         `dataset.target` object.\n127 | \n128 |         .. versionadded:: 0.20\n129 | \n130 |     Returns\n131 |     -------\n132 |     dataset : :class:`~sklearn.utils.Bunch`\n133 |         Dictionary-like object, with the following attributes.\n134 | \n135 |         data : scipy csr array, dtype np.float64, shape (804414, 47236)\n136 |             The array has 0.16% of non zero values.\n137 |         target : scipy csr array, dtype np.uint8, shape (804414, 103)\n138 |             Each sample has a value of 1 in its categories, and 0 in others.\n139 |             The array has 3.15% of non zero values.\n140 |         sample_id : numpy array, dtype np.uint32, shape (804414,)\n141 |             Identification number of each sample, as ordered in dataset.data.\n142 |         target_names : numpy array, dtype object, length (103)\n143 |             Names of each target (RCV1 topics), as ordered in dataset.target.\n144 |         DESCR : string\n145 |             Description of the RCV1 dataset.\n146 | \n147 |     (data, target) : tuple if ``return_X_y`` is True\n148 | \n149 |         .. versionadded:: 0.20\n150 |     \"\"\"\n151 |     N_SAMPLES = 804414\n152 |     N_FEATURES = 47236\n153 |     N_CATEGORIES = 103\n154 |     N_TRAIN = 23149\n155 | \n156 |     data_home = get_data_home(data_home=data_home)\n157 |     rcv1_dir = join(data_home, \"RCV1\")\n158 |     if download_if_missing:\n159 |         if not exists(rcv1_dir):\n160 |             makedirs(rcv1_dir)\n161 | \n162 |     samples_path = _pkl_filepath(rcv1_dir, \"samples.pkl\")\n163 |     sample_id_path = _pkl_filepath(rcv1_dir, \"sample_id.pkl\")\n164 |     sample_topics_path = _pkl_filepath(rcv1_dir, \"sample_topics.pkl\")\n165 |     topics_path = _pkl_filepath(rcv1_dir, \"topics_names.pkl\")\n166 | \n167 |     # load data (X) and sample_id\n168 |     if download_if_missing and (not exists(samples_path) or\n169 |                                 not exists(sample_id_path)):\n170 |         files = []\n171 |         for each in XY_METADATA:\n172 |             logger.info(\"Downloading %s\" % each.url)\n173 |             file_path = _fetch_remote(each, dirname=rcv1_dir)\n174 |             files.append(GzipFile(filename=file_path))\n175 | \n176 |         Xy = load_svmlight_files(files, n_features=N_FEATURES)\n177 | \n178 |         # Training data is before testing data\n179 |         X = sp.vstack([Xy[8], Xy[0], Xy[2], Xy[4], Xy[6]]).tocsr()\n180 |         sample_id = np.hstack((Xy[9], Xy[1], Xy[3], Xy[5], Xy[7]))\n181 |         sample_id = sample_id.astype(np.uint32, copy=False)\n182 | \n183 |         joblib.dump(X, samples_path, compress=9)\n184 |         joblib.dump(sample_id, sample_id_path, compress=9)\n185 | \n186 |         # delete archives\n187 |         for f in files:\n188 |             f.close()\n189 |             remove(f.name)\n190 |     else:\n191 |         X = joblib.load(samples_path)\n192 |         sample_id = joblib.load(sample_id_path)\n193 | \n194 |     # load target (y), categories, and sample_id_bis\n195 |     if download_if_missing and (not exists(sample_topics_path) or\n196 |                                 not exists(topics_path)):\n197 |         logger.info(\"Downloading %s\" % TOPICS_METADATA.url)\n198 |         topics_archive_path = _fetch_remote(TOPICS_METADATA,\n199 |                                             dirname=rcv1_dir)\n200 | \n201 |         # parse the target file\n202 |         n_cat = -1\n203 |         n_doc = -1\n204 |         doc_previous = -1\n205 |         y = np.zeros((N_SAMPLES, N_CATEGORIES), dtype=np.uint8)\n206 |         sample_id_bis = np.zeros(N_SAMPLES, dtype=np.int32)\n207 |         category_names = {}\n208 |         with GzipFile(filename=topics_archive_path, mode='rb') as f:\n209 |             for line in f:\n210 |                 line_components = line.decode(\"ascii\").split(\" \")\n211 |                 if len(line_components) == 3:\n212 |                     cat, doc, _ = line_components\n213 |                     if cat not in category_names:\n214 |                         n_cat += 1\n215 |                         category_names[cat] = n_cat\n216 | \n217 |                     doc = int(doc)\n218 |                     if doc != doc_previous:\n219 |                         doc_previous = doc\n220 |                         n_doc += 1\n221 |                         sample_id_bis[n_doc] = doc\n222 |                     y[n_doc, category_names[cat]] = 1\n223 | \n224 |         # delete archive\n225 |         remove(topics_archive_path)\n226 | \n227 |         # Samples in X are ordered with sample_id,\n228 |         # whereas in y, they are ordered with sample_id_bis.\n229 |         permutation = _find_permutation(sample_id_bis, sample_id)\n230 |         y = y[permutation, :]\n231 | \n232 |         # save category names in a list, with same order than y\n233 |         categories = np.empty(N_CATEGORIES, dtype=object)\n234 |         for k in category_names.keys():\n235 |             categories[category_names[k]] = k\n236 | \n237 |         # reorder categories in lexicographic order\n238 |         order = np.argsort(categories)\n239 |         categories = categories[order]\n240 |         y = sp.csr_matrix(y[:, order])\n241 | \n242 |         joblib.dump(y, sample_topics_path, compress=9)\n243 |         joblib.dump(categories, topics_path, compress=9)\n244 |     else:\n245 |         y = joblib.load(sample_topics_path)\n246 |         categories = joblib.load(topics_path)\n247 | \n248 |     if subset == 'all':\n249 |         pass\n250 |     elif subset == 'train':\n251 |         X = X[:N_TRAIN, :]\n252 |         y = y[:N_TRAIN, :]\n253 |         sample_id = sample_id[:N_TRAIN]\n254 |     elif subset == 'test':\n255 |         X = X[N_TRAIN:, :]\n256 |         y = y[N_TRAIN:, :]\n257 |         sample_id = sample_id[N_TRAIN:]\n258 |     else:\n259 |         raise ValueError(\"Unknown subset parameter. Got '%s' instead of one\"\n260 |                          \" of ('all', 'train', test')\" % subset)\n261 | \n262 |     if shuffle:\n263 |         X, y, sample_id = shuffle_(X, y, sample_id, random_state=random_state)\n264 | \n265 |     module_path = dirname(__file__)\n266 |     with open(join(module_path, 'descr', 'rcv1.rst')) as rst_file:\n267 |         fdescr = rst_file.read()\n268 | \n269 |     if return_X_y:\n270 |         return X, y\n271 | \n272 |     return Bunch(data=X, target=y, sample_id=sample_id,\n273 |                  target_names=categories, DESCR=fdescr)\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/cluster/_dbscan.py:DBSCAN.fit`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n288 | def fit(self, X, y=None, sample_weight=None):\n289 |         \"\"\"Perform DBSCAN clustering from features, or distance matrix.\n290 | \n291 |         Parameters\n292 |         ----------\n293 |         X : {array-like, sparse matrix} of shape (n_samples, n_features), or \\\n294 |             (n_samples, n_samples)\n295 |             Training instances to cluster, or distances between instances if\n296 |             ``metric='precomputed'``. If a sparse matrix is provided, it will\n297 |             be converted into a sparse ``csr_matrix``.\n298 | \n299 |         sample_weight : array-like of shape (n_samples,), default=None\n300 |             Weight of each sample, such that a sample with a weight of at least\n301 |             ``min_samples`` is by itself a core sample; a sample with a\n302 |             negative weight may inhibit its eps-neighbor from being core.\n303 |             Note that weights are absolute, and default to 1.\n304 | \n305 |         y : Ignored\n306 |             Not used, present here for API consistency by convention.\n307 | \n308 |         Returns\n309 |         -------\n310 |         self\n311 | \n312 |         \"\"\"\n313 |         X = self._validate_data(X, accept_sparse='csr')\n314 | \n315 |         if not self.eps > 0.0:\n316 |             raise ValueError(\"eps must be positive.\")\n317 | \n318 |         if sample_weight is not None:\n319 |             sample_weight = _check_sample_weight(sample_weight, X)\n320 | \n321 |         # Calculate neighborhood for all samples. This leaves the original\n322 |         # point in, which needs to be considered later (i.e. point i is in the\n323 |         # neighborhood of point i. While True, its useless information)\n324 |         if self.metric == 'precomputed' and sparse.issparse(X):\n325 |             # set the diagonal to explicit values, as a point is its own\n326 |             # neighbor\n327 |             with warnings.catch_warnings():\n328 |                 warnings.simplefilter('ignore', sparse.SparseEfficiencyWarning)\n329 |                 X.setdiag(X.diagonal())  # XXX: modifies X's internals in-place\n330 | \n331 |         neighbors_model = NearestNeighbors(\n332 |             radius=self.eps, algorithm=self.algorithm,\n333 |             leaf_size=self.leaf_size, metric=self.metric,\n334 |             metric_params=self.metric_params, p=self.p, n_jobs=self.n_jobs)\n335 |         neighbors_model.fit(X)\n336 |         # This has worst case O(n^2) memory complexity\n337 |         neighborhoods = neighbors_model.radius_neighbors(X,\n338 |                                                          return_distance=False)\n339 | \n340 |         if sample_weight is None:\n341 |             n_neighbors = np.array([len(neighbors)\n342 |                                     for neighbors in neighborhoods])\n343 |         else:\n344 |             n_neighbors = np.array([np.sum(sample_weight[neighbors])\n345 |                                     for neighbors in neighborhoods])\n346 | \n347 |         # Initially, all samples are noise.\n348 |         labels = np.full(X.shape[0], -1, dtype=np.intp)\n349 | \n350 |         # A list of all core samples found.\n351 |         core_samples = np.asarray(n_neighbors >= self.min_samples,\n352 |                                   dtype=np.uint8)\n353 |         dbscan_inner(core_samples, neighborhoods, labels)\n354 | \n355 |         self.core_sample_indices_ = np.where(core_samples)[0]\n356 |         self.labels_ = labels\n357 | \n358 |         if len(self.core_sample_indices_):\n359 |             # fix for scipy sparse indexing issue\n360 |             self.components_ = X[self.core_sample_indices_].copy()\n361 |         else:\n362 |             # no core samples\n363 |             self.components_ = np.empty((0, X.shape[1]))\n364 |         return self\n```\n"}, {"content": "Let me get more details about the data download and checksum verification:\n\n<function=get_entity_contents>\n<parameter=entity_names>['sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_download_data_to_bunch', 'sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_get_data_description_by_id']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "get_entity_contents", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for entity `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_download_data_to_bunch`...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_download_data_to_bunch`.\nSource: Exact match found for entity name `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_download_data_to_bunch`.\n```\n509 | def _download_data_to_bunch(\n510 |     url: str,\n511 |     sparse: bool,\n512 |     data_home: Optional[str],\n513 |     *,\n514 |     as_frame: bool,\n515 |     features_list: List,\n516 |     data_columns: List[int],\n517 |     target_columns: List,\n518 |     shape: Optional[Tuple[int, int]]\n519 | ):\n520 |     \"\"\"Download OpenML ARFF and convert to Bunch of data\n521 |     \"\"\"\n522 |     # NB: this function is long in order to handle retry for any failure\n523 |     #     during the streaming parse of the ARFF.\n524 | \n525 |     # Prepare which columns and data types should be returned for the X and y\n526 |     features_dict = {feature['name']: feature for feature in features_list}\n527 | \n528 |     # XXX: col_slice_y should be all nominal or all numeric\n529 |     _verify_target_data_type(features_dict, target_columns)\n530 | \n531 |     col_slice_y = [int(features_dict[col_name]['index'])\n532 |                    for col_name in target_columns]\n533 | \n534 |     col_slice_x = [int(features_dict[col_name]['index'])\n535 |                    for col_name in data_columns]\n536 |     for col_idx in col_slice_y:\n537 |         feat = features_list[col_idx]\n538 |         nr_missing = int(feat['number_of_missing_values'])\n539 |         if nr_missing > 0:\n540 |             raise ValueError('Target column {} has {} missing values. '\n541 |                              'Missing values are not supported for target '\n542 |                              'columns. '.format(feat['name'], nr_missing))\n543 | \n544 |     # Access an ARFF file on the OpenML server. Documentation:\n545 |     # https://www.openml.org/api_data_docs#!/data/get_download_id\n546 | \n547 |     if sparse is True:\n548 |         return_type = _arff.COO\n549 |     else:\n550 |         return_type = _arff.DENSE_GEN\n551 | \n552 |     frame = nominal_attributes = None\n553 | \n554 |     parse_arff: Callable\n555 |     postprocess: Callable\n556 |     if as_frame:\n557 |         columns = data_columns + target_columns\n558 |         parse_arff = partial(_convert_arff_data_dataframe, columns=columns,\n559 |                              features_dict=features_dict)\n560 | \n561 |         def postprocess(frame):\n562 |             X = frame[data_columns]\n563 |             if len(target_columns) >= 2:\n564 |                 y = frame[target_columns]\n565 |             elif len(target_columns) == 1:\n566 |                 y = frame[target_columns[0]]\n567 |             else:\n568 |                 y = None\n569 |             return X, y, frame, nominal_attributes\n570 |     else:\n571 |         def parse_arff(arff):\n572 |             X, y = _convert_arff_data(arff, col_slice_x, col_slice_y, shape)\n573 |             # nominal attributes is a dict mapping from the attribute name to\n574 |             # the possible values. Includes also the target column (which will\n575 |             # be popped off below, before it will be packed in the Bunch\n576 |             # object)\n577 |             nominal_attributes = {k: v for k, v in arff['attributes']\n578 |                                   if isinstance(v, list) and\n579 |                                   k in data_columns + target_columns}\n580 |             return X, y, nominal_attributes\n581 | \n582 |         def postprocess(X, y, nominal_attributes):\n583 |             is_classification = {col_name in nominal_attributes\n584 |                                  for col_name in target_columns}\n585 |             if not is_classification:\n586 |                 # No target\n587 |                 pass\n588 |             elif all(is_classification):\n589 |                 y = np.hstack([\n590 |                     np.take(\n591 |                         np.asarray(nominal_attributes.pop(col_name),\n592 |                                    dtype='O'),\n593 |                         y[:, i:i + 1].astype(int, copy=False))\n594 |                     for i, col_name in enumerate(target_columns)\n595 |                 ])\n596 |             elif any(is_classification):\n597 |                 raise ValueError('Mix of nominal and non-nominal targets is '\n598 |                                  'not currently supported')\n599 | \n600 |             # reshape y back to 1-D array, if there is only 1 target column;\n601 |             # back to None if there are not target columns\n602 |             if y.shape[1] == 1:\n603 |                 y = y.reshape((-1,))\n604 |             elif y.shape[1] == 0:\n605 |                 y = None\n606 |             return X, y, frame, nominal_attributes\n607 | \n608 |     out = _retry_with_clean_cache(url, data_home)(\n609 |         _load_arff_response)(url, data_home,\n610 |                              return_type=return_type,\n611 |                              encode_nominal=not as_frame,\n612 |                              parse_arff=parse_arff)\n613 |     X, y, frame, nominal_attributes = postprocess(*out)\n614 | \n615 |     return Bunch(data=X, target=y, frame=frame,\n616 |                  categories=nominal_attributes,\n617 |                  feature_names=data_columns,\n618 |                  target_names=target_columns)\n```\n\n\n##Searching for entity `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_get_data_description_by_id`...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_get_data_description_by_id`.\nSource: Exact match found for entity name `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_get_data_description_by_id`.\n```\n430 | def _get_data_description_by_id(\n431 |     data_id: int, data_home: Optional[str]\n432 | ) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n433 |     # OpenML API function: https://www.openml.org/api_docs#!/data/get_data_id\n434 |     url = _DATA_INFO.format(data_id)\n435 |     error_message = \"Dataset with data_id {} not found.\".format(data_id)\n436 |     json_data = _get_json_content_from_openml_api(\n437 |         url, error_message, data_home=data_home\n438 |     )\n439 |     return json_data['data_set_description']\n```\n"}, {"content": "Let me check the retry and response handling:\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>['_retry_with_clean_cache', '_load_arff_response']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for term \"_retry_with_clean_cache\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_retry_with_clean_cache`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `_retry_with_clean_cache`.\n```\n47 | def _retry_with_clean_cache(\n48 |     openml_path: str, data_home: Optional[str]\n49 | ) -> Callable:\n50 |     \"\"\"If the first call to the decorated function fails, the local cached\n51 |     file is removed, and the function is called again. If ``data_home`` is\n52 |     ``None``, then the function is called once.\n53 |     \"\"\"\n54 |     def decorator(f):\n55 |         @wraps(f)\n56 |         def wrapper(*args, **kw):\n57 |             if data_home is None:\n58 |                 return f(*args, **kw)\n59 |             try:\n60 |                 return f(*args, **kw)\n61 |             except HTTPError:\n62 |                 raise\n63 |             except Exception:\n64 |                 warn(\"Invalid cache, redownloading file\", RuntimeWarning)\n65 |                 local_path = _get_local_path(openml_path, data_home)\n66 |                 if os.path.exists(local_path):\n67 |                     os.unlink(local_path)\n68 |                 return f(*args, **kw)\n69 |         return wrapper\n70 |     return decorator\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"_load_arff_response\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_load_arff_response`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `_load_arff_response`.\n```\n491 | def _load_arff_response(\n492 |     url: str,\n493 |     data_home: Optional[str],\n494 |     return_type, encode_nominal: bool,\n495 |     parse_arff: Callable[[ArffContainerType], Tuple]\n496 | ) -> Tuple:\n497 |     \"\"\"Load arff data with url and parses arff response with parse_arff\"\"\"\n498 |     response = _open_openml_url(url, data_home)\n499 | \n500 |     with closing(response):\n501 |         # Note that if the data is dense, no reading is done until the data\n502 |         # generator is iterated.\n503 |         arff = _arff.load((line.decode('utf-8') for line in response),\n504 |                           return_type=return_type,\n505 |                           encode_nominal=encode_nominal)\n506 |         return parse_arff(arff)\n```\n"}, {"content": "Based on my analysis, I can now identify the relevant code locations for implementing MD5 checksum validation in `fetch_openml`. Here's the output in the requested format:\n\n```\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nline: 430-439\nfunction: _get_data_description_by_id\n# This function retrieves the dataset description from OpenML which contains the md5_checksum\n\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nline: 608-612\nfunction: _download_data_to_bunch\n# This is where the data is actually downloaded and parsed\n\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nline: 491-506\nfunction: _load_arff_response\n# Handles the actual data loading from the downloaded file\n\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nline: 47-70\nfunction: _retry_with_clean_cache\n# Handles cache management and retries\n\nsklearn/utils/validation.py\nline: 1344-1379\nfunction: _check_fit_params\n# General validation utilities that could be useful for implementing checksum checks\n```\n\nThese locations form the core workflow for fetching and processing OpenML data sets. The issue requests adding MD5 checksum validation, which would primarily involve modifying the data download and processing flow to include checksum verification at the appropriate point.\n\n<function=finish>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}], "tools": [{"type": "function", "function": {"name": "finish", "description": "Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task."}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "search_code_snippets", "description": "Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the 