    colormap color (see `.Colormap.set_bad`).\n4791 | \n4792 |         Returns\n4793 |         -------\n4794 |         `~matplotlib.collections.PathCollection`\n4795 | \n4796 |         Other Parameters\n4797 |         ----------------\n4798 |         data : indexable object, optional\n4799 |             DATA_PARAMETER_PLACEHOLDER\n4800 |         **kwargs : `~matplotlib.collections.Collection` properties\n4801 | \n4802 |         See Also\n4803 |         --------\n4804 |         plot : To plot scatter plots when markers are identical in size and\n4805 |             color.\n4806 | \n4807 |         Notes\n4808 |         -----\n4809 |         * The `.plot` function will be faster for scatterplots where markers\n4810 |           don't vary in size or color.\n4811 | \n4812 |         * Any or all of *x*, *y*, *s*, and *c* may be masked arrays, in which\n4813 |           case all masks will be combined and only unmasked points will be\n4814 |           plotted.\n4815 | \n4816 |         * Fundamentally, scatter works with 1D arrays; *x*, *y*, *s*, and *c*\n4817 |           may be input as N-D arrays, but within scatter they will be\n4818 |           flattened. The exception is *c*, which will be flattened only if its\n4819 |           size matches the size of *x* and *y*.\n4820 | \n4821 |         \"\"\"\n4822 |         # add edgecolors and linewidths to kwargs so they\n4823 |         # can be processed by normailze_kwargs\n4824 |         if edgecolors is not None:\n4825 |             kwargs.update({'edgecolors': edgecolors})\n4826 |         if linewidths is not None:\n4827 |             kwargs.update({'linewidths': linewidths})\n4828 | \n4829 |         kwargs = cbook.normalize_kwargs(kwargs, mcoll.Collection)\n4830 |         # re direct linewidth and edgecolor so it can be\n4831 |         # further processed by the rest of the function\n4832 |         linewidths = kwargs.pop('linewidth', None)\n4833 |         edgecolors = kwargs.pop('edgecolor', None)\n4834 |         # Proces