",\n2542 |     ) -> float | Series:\n2543 |         \"\"\"\n2544 |         Return value at the given quantile.\n2545 | \n2546 |         Parameters\n2547 |         ----------\n2548 |         q : float or array-like, default 0.5 (50% quantile)\n2549 |             The quantile(s) to compute, which can lie in range: 0 <= q <= 1.\n2550 |         interpolation : {'linear', 'lower', 'higher', 'midpoint', 'nearest'}\n2551 |             This optional parameter specifies the interpolation method to use,\n2552 |             when the desired quantile lies between two data points `i` and `j`:\n2553 | \n2554 |                 * linear: `i + (j - i) * (x-i)/(j-i)`, where `(x-i)/(j-i)` is\n2555 |                   the fractional part of the index surrounded by `i > j`.\n2556 |                 * lower: `i`.\n2557 |                 * higher: `j`.\n2558 |                 * nearest: `i` or `j` whichever is nearest.\n2559 |                 * midpoint: (`i` + `j`) / 2.\n2560 | \n2561 |         Returns\n2562 |         -------\n2563 |         float or Series\n2564 |             If ``q`` is an array, a Series will be returned where the\n2565 |             index is ``q`` and the values are the quantiles, otherwise\n2566 |             a float will be returned.\n2567 | \n2568 |         See Also\n2569 |         --------\n2570 |         core.window.Rolling.quantile : Calculate the rolling quantile.\n2571 |         numpy.percentile : Returns the q-th percentile(s) of the array elements.\n2572 | \n2573 |         Examples\n2574 |         --------\n2575 |         >>> s = pd.Series([1, 2, 3, 4])\n2576 |         >>> s.quantile(0.5)\n2577 |         2.5\n2578 |         >>> s.quantile([0.25, 0.5, 0.75])\n2579 |         0.25    1.75\n2580 |         0.50    2.50\n2581 |         0.75    3.25\n2582 |         dtype: float64\n2583 |         \"\"\"\n2584 |         validate_percentile(q)\n2585 | \n2586 |         # We dispatch to DataFrame so that core.internals only has to worry\n2587 |         #  about 2D cases