argument because\n44 |         that is set to the color argument. If provided, it will be overridden.\n45 | \n46 |     Returns\n47 |     -------\n48 |     matplotlib.collections.LineCollection\n49 |         The generated line collection representing the colored line.\n50 |     \"\"\"\n51 |     if \"array\" in lc_kwargs:\n52 |         warnings.warn('The provided \"array\" keyword argument will be overridden')\n53 | \n54 |     # Default the capstyle to butt so that the line segments smoothly line up\n55 |     default_kwargs = {\"capstyle\": \"butt\"}\n56 |     default_kwargs.update(lc_kwargs)\n57 | \n58 |     # Compute the midpoints of the line segments. Include the first and last points\n59 |     # twice so we don't need any special syntax later to handle them.\n60 |     x = np.asarray(x)\n61 |     y = np.asarray(y)\n62 |     x_midpts = np.hstack((x[0], 0.5 * (x[1:] + x[:-1]), x[-1]))\n63 |     y_midpts = np.hstack((y[0], 0.5 * (y[1:] + y[:-1]), y[-1]))\n64 | \n65 |     # Determine the start, middle, and end coordinate pair of each line segment.\n66 |     # Use the reshape to add an extra dimension so each pair of points is in its\n67 |     # own list. Then concatenate them to create:\n68 |     # [\n69 |     #   [(x1_start, y1_start), (x1_mid, y1_mid), (x1_end, y1_end)],\n70 |     #   [(x2_start, y2_start), (x2_mid, y2_mid), (x2_end, y2_end)],\n71 |     #   ...\n72 |     # ]\n73 |     coord_start = np.column_stack((x_midpts[:-1], y_midpts[:-1]))[:, np.newaxis, :]\n74 |     coord_mid = np.column_stack((x, y))[:, np.newaxis, :]\n75 |     coord_end = np.column_stack((x_midpts[1:], y_midpts[1:]))[:, np.newaxis, :]\n76 |     segments = np.concatenate((coord_start, coord_mid, coord_end), axis=1)\n77 | \n78 |     lc = LineCollection(segments, **default_kwargs)\n79 |     lc.set_array(c)  # set the colors of each segment\n80 | \n81 |     return ax.add_collection(lc)\n```\n\nFound function `galleries/examples/lines_bars_and_markers/multicolored_line.py:colored_line_between_pts`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n125 | def colored_line_between_pts(x, y, c, ax, **lc_kwargs):\n126 |     \"\"\"\n127 |     Plot a line with a color specified between (x, y) points by a third value.\n128 | \n129 |     It does this by creating a collection of line segments between each pair of\n130 |     neighboring points. The color of each segment is determined by the\n131 |     made up of two straight lines each connecting the current (x, y) point to the\n132 |     midpoints of the lines connecting the current point with its two neighbors.\n133 |     This creates a smooth line with no gaps between the line segments.\n134 | \n135 |     Parameters\n136 |     ----------\n137 |     x, y : array-like\n138 |         The horizontal and vertical coordinates of the data points.\n139 |     c : array-like\n140 |         The color values, which should have a size one less than that of x and y.\n141 |     ax : Axes\n142 |         Axis object on which to plot the colored line.\n143 |     **lc_kwargs\n144 |         Any additional arguments to pass to matplotlib.collections.LineCollection\n145 |         constructor. This should not include the array keyword argument because\n146 |         that is set to the color argument. If provided, it will be overridden.\n147 | \n148 |     Returns\n149 |     -------\n150 |     matplotlib.collections.LineCollection\n151 |         The generated line collection representing the colored line.\n152 |     \"\"\"\n153 |     if \"array\" in lc_kwargs:\n154 |         warnings.warn('The provided \"array\" keyword argument will be overridden')\n155 | \n156 |     # Check color array size (LineCollection still works, but values are unused)\n157 |     if len(c) != len(x) - 1:\n158 |         warnings.warn(\n159 |             \"The c argument should have a length one less than the length of x and y. \"\n160 |             \"If it has the same length, use the colored_line function instead.\"\n161 |         )\n162 | \n163 |     # Create a set of line segments so that we can color them individually\n164 |     # This creates the points as an N x 1 x 2 array so that we can stack points\n165 |     # together easily to get the segments. The segments array for line collection\n166 |     # needs to be (numlines) x (points per line) x 2 (for x and y)\n167 |     points = np.array([x, y]).T.reshape(-1, 1, 2)\n168 |     segments = np.concatenate([points[:-1], points[1:]], axis=1)\n169 |     lc = LineCollection(segments, **lc_kwargs)\n170 | \n171 |     # Set the values used for colormapping\n172 |     lc.set_array(c)\n173 | \n174 |     return ax.add_collection(lc)\n```\n"}, {"content": "Let me get the specific content of the LineCollection class to analyze the docstring:\n\n<function=get_entity_contents>\n<parameter=entity_names>['lib/matplotlib/collections.py:LineCollection']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "get_entity_contents", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for entity `lib/matplotlib/collections.py:LineCollection`...\n### Search Result:\nFound class `lib/matplotlib/collections.py:LineCollection`.\nSource: Exact match found for entity name `lib/matplotlib/collections.py:LineCollection`.\n```\n1587 | class LineCollection(Collection):\n1588 |     r\"\"\"\n1589 |     Represents a sequence of `.Line2D`\\s that should be drawn together.\n1590 | \n1591 |     This class extends `.Collection` to represent a sequence of\n1592 |     `.Line2D`\\s instead of just a sequence of `.Patch`\\s.\n1593 |     Just as in `.Collection`, each property of a *LineCollection* may be either\n1594 |     a single value or a list of values. This list is then used cyclically for\n1595 |     each element of the LineCollection, so the property of the ``i``\\th element\n1596 |     of the collection is::\n1597 | \n1598 |       prop[i % len(prop)]\n1599 | \n1600 |     The properties of each member of a *LineCollection* default to their values\n1601 |     in :rc:`lines.*` instead of :rc:`patch.*`, and the property *colors* is\n1602 |     added in place of *edgecolors*.\n1603 |     \"\"\"\n1604 | \n1605 |     _edge_default = True\n1606 | \n1607 |     def __init__(self, segments,  # Can be None.\n1608 |                  *,\n1609 |                  zorder=2,        # Collection.zorder is 1\n1610 |                  **kwargs\n1611 |                  ):\n1612 |         \"\"\"\n1613 |         Parameters\n1614 |         ----------\n1615 |         segments : list of array-like\n1616 |             A sequence (*line0*, *line1*, *line2*) of lines, where each line is a list\n1617 |             of points::\n1618 | \n1619 |                 lineN = [(x0, y0), (x1, y1), ... (xm, ym)]\n1620 | \n1621 |             or the equivalent Mx2 numpy array with two columns. Each line\n1622 |             can have a different number of segments.\n1623 |         linewidths : float or list of float, default: :rc:`lines.linewidth`\n1624 |             The width of each line in points.\n1625 |         colors : :mpltype:`color` or list of color, default: :rc:`lines.color`\n1626 |             A sequence of RGBA tuples (e.g., arbitrary color strings, etc, not\n1627 |             allowed).\n1628 |         antialiaseds : bool or list of bool, default: :rc:`lines.antialiased`\n1629 |             Whether to use antialiasing for each line.\n1630 |         zorder : float, default: 2\n1631 |             zorder of the lines once drawn.\n1632 | \n1633 |         facecolors : :mpltype:`color` or list of :mpltype:`color`, default: 'none'\n1634 |             When setting *facecolors*, each line is interpreted as a boundary\n1635 |             for an area, implicitly closing the path from the last point to the\n1636 |             first point. The enclosed area is filled with *facecolor*.\n1637 |             In order to manually specify what should count as the \"interior\" of\n1638 |             each line, please use `.PathCollection` instead, where the\n1639 |             \"interior\" can be specified by appropriate usage of\n1640 |             `~.path.Path.CLOSEPOLY`.\n1641 | \n1642 |         **kwargs\n1643 |             Forwarded to `.Collection`.\n1644 |         \"\"\"\n1645 |         # Unfortunately, mplot3d needs this explicit setting of 'facecolors'.\n1646 |         kwargs.setdefault('facecolors', 'none')\n1647 |         super().__init__(\n1648 |             zorder=zorder,\n1649 |             **kwargs)\n1650 |         self.set_segments(segments)\n1651 | \n1652 |     def set_segments(self, segments):\n1653 |         if segments is None:\n1654 |             return\n1655 | \n1656 |         self._paths = [mpath.Path(seg) if isinstance(seg, np.ma.MaskedArray)\n1657 |                        else mpath.Path(np.asarray(seg, float))\n1658 |                        for seg in segments]\n1659 |         self.stale = True\n1660 | \n1661 |     set_verts = set_segments  # for compatibility with PolyCollection\n1662 |     set_paths = set_segments\n1663 | \n1664 |     def get_segments(self):\n1665 |         \"\"\"\n1666 |         Returns\n1667 |         -------\n1668 |         list\n1669 |             List of segments in the LineCollection. Each list item contains an\n1670 |             array of vertices.\n1671 |         \"\"\"\n1672 |         segments = []\n1673 | \n1674 |         for path in self._paths:\n1675 |             vertices = [\n1676 |                 vertex\n1677 |                 for vertex, _\n1678 |                 # Never simplify here, we want to get the data-space values\n1679 |                 # back and there in no way to know the \"right\" simplification\n1680 |                 # threshold so never try.\n1681 |                 in path.iter_segments(simplify=False)\n1682 |             ]\n1683 |             vertices = np.asarray(vertices)\n1684 |             segments.append(vertices)\n1685 | \n1686 |         return segments\n1687 | \n1688 |     def _get_default_linewidth(self):\n1689 |         return mpl.rcParams['lines.linewidth']\n1690 | \n1691 |     def _get_default_antialiased(self):\n1692 |         return mpl.rcParams['lines.antialiased']\n1693 | \n1694 |     def _get_default_edgecolor(self):\n1695 |         return mpl.rcParams['lines.color']\n1696 | \n1697 |     def _get_default_facecolor(self):\n1698 |         return 'none'\n1699 | \n1700 |     def set_alpha(self, alpha):\n1701 |         # docstring inherited\n1702 |         super().set_alpha(alpha)\n1703 |         if self._gapcolor is not None:\n1704 |             self.set_gapcolor(self._original_gapcolor)\n1705 | \n1706 |     def set_color(self, c):\n1707 |         \"\"\"\n1708 |         Set the edgecolor(s) of the LineCollection.\n1709 | \n1710 |         Parameters\n1711 |         ----------\n1712 |         c : :mpltype:`color` or list of :mpltype:`color`\n1713 |             Single color (all lines have same color), or a\n1714 |             sequence of RGBA tuples; if it is a sequence the lines will\n1715 |             cycle through the sequence.\n1716 |         \"\"\"\n1717 |         self.set_edgecolor(c)\n1718 | \n1719 |     set_colors = set_color\n1720 | \n1721 |     def get_color(self):\n1722 |         return self._edgecolors\n1723 | \n1724 |     get_colors = get_color  # for compatibility with old versions\n1725 | \n1726 |     def set_gapcolor(self, gapcolor):\n1727 |         \"\"\"\n1728 |         Set a color to fill the gaps in the dashed line style.\n1729 | \n1730 |         .. note::\n1731 | \n1732 |             Striped lines are created by drawing two interleaved dashed lines.\n1733 |             There can be overlaps between those two, which may result in\n1734 |             artifacts when using transparency.\n1735 | \n1736 |             This functionality is experimental and may change.\n1737 | \n1738 |         Parameters\n1739 |         ----------\n1740 |         gapcolor : :mpltype:`color` or list of :mpltype:`color` or None\n1741 |             The color with which to fill the gaps. If None, the gaps are\n1742 |             unfilled.\n1743 |         \"\"\"\n1744 |         self._original_gapcolor = gapcolor\n1745 |         self._set_gapcolor(gapcolor)\n1746 | \n1747 |     def _set_gapcolor(self, gapcolor):\n1748 |         if gapcolor is not None:\n1749 |             gapcolor = mcolors.to_rgba_array(gapcolor, self._alpha)\n1750 |         self._gapcolor = gapcolor\n1751 |         self.stale = True\n1752 | \n1753 |     def get_gapcolor(self):\n1754 |         return self._gapcolor\n1755 | \n1756 |     def _get_inverse_paths_linestyles(self):\n1757 |         \"\"\"\n1758 |         Returns the path and pattern for the gaps in the non-solid lines.\n1759 | \n1760 |         This path and pattern is the inverse of the path and pattern used to\n1761 |         construct the non-solid lines. For solid lines, we set the inverse path\n1762 |         to nans to prevent drawing an inverse line.\n1763 |         \"\"\"\n1764 |         path_patterns = [\n1765 |             (mpath.Path(np.full((1, 2), np.nan)), ls)\n1766 |             if ls == (0, None) else\n1767 |             (path, mlines._get_inverse_dash_pattern(*ls))\n1768 |             for (path, ls) in\n1769 |             zip(self._paths, itertools.cycle(self._linestyles))]\n1770 | \n1771 |         return zip(*path_patterns)\n```\n"}, {"content": "After analyzing the issue and the code, I can now provide the locations that need modification to implement the requested feature improvement. Here's the final output with the locations ordered by importance:\n\n```\nlib/matplotlib/collections.py\nline: 1615-1622\nclass: LineCollection\nfunction: __init__\n\nlib/matplotlib/collections.py\nline: 1669-1686\nclass: LineCollection\nfunction: get_segments\n\nlib/matplotlib/collections.py\nline: 1656-1659\nclass: LineCollection\nfunction: set_segments\n\nlib/matplotlib/collections.py\nline: 1764-1771\nclass: LineCollection\nfunction: _get_inverse_paths_linestyles\n\nlib/matplotlib/examples/lines_bars_and_markers/multicolored_line.py\nline: 24-81\nfunction: colored_line\n```\n\nThe main changes needed are focused on the `LineCollection` class in `matplotlib/collections.py`. The most important modifications are:\n\n1. The primary docstring of `LineCollection.__init__` needs updating to reflect the new format description.\n2. The `get_segments` method documentation needs to match the new format description.\n3. The `set_segments` method implementation confirms the accepted format.\n4. The `_get_inverse_paths_linestyles` method continues to use the old format but confirms it works with both formats.\n5. The example `colored_line` function demonstrates the usage of the class and should be updated to match the new docstring format.\n\nThese locations represent represent a complete view of the `LineCollection` class documentation and implementation that needs to be consistent with the proposed changes.\n\n<function=finish>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}], "tools": [{"type": "function", "function": {"name": "finish", "description": "Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task."}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "search_code_snippets", "description": "Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"search_terms": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search."}, "line_nums": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "integer"}, "description": "Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search."}, "file_path_or_pattern": {"type": "string", "description": "A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.", "default": "**/*.py"}}, "required": []}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_entity_contents", "description": "\nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"entity_names": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py')."}}, "required": ["entity_names"]}}}], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 24633, "completion_tokens": 455}, "time": 77.0426561832428}]}}
{"instance_id": "pandas-dev__pandas-60415", "found_files": [["pandas/core/generic.py"]], "found_modules": [["asv_bench/benchmarks/groupby.py:MultipleCategories", "pandas/core/arrays/arrow/array.py:ArrowExtensionArray", "pandas/core/apply.py:FrameApply", "pandas/io/formats/info.py:_TableBuilderVerboseMixin", "asv_bench/benchmarks/frame_methods.py:FindValidIndex", "asv_bench/benchmarks/groupby.py:Datelike", "asv_bench/benchmarks/frame_methods.py:Update", "asv_bench/benchmarks/groupby.py:SumMultiLevel", "pandas/core/arrays/masked.py:BaseMaskedArray", "pandas/core/dtypes/dtypes.py:DatetimeTZDtype", "asv_bench/benchmarks/groupby.py:SumTimeDelta", "pandas/core/groupby/grouper.py:_is_label_like", "pandas/core/arrays/period.py:PeriodArray", "pandas/core/series.py:Series", "pandas/core/internals/blocks.py:Block", "pandas/core/window/rolling.py:BaseWindowGroupby", "asv_bench/benchmarks/groupby.py:SumBools", "pandas/io/stata.py:StataMissingValue", "pandas/core/arrays/string_.py:StringArray", "pandas/core/arrays/datetimelike.py:DatetimeLikeArrayMixin", "pandas/conftest.py:ea_scalar_and_dtype", "pandas/core/generic.py:NDFrame"]], "found_entities": [["asv_bench/benchmarks/groupby.py:MultipleCategories.time_groupby_extra_cat_sort", "pandas/core/arrays/arrow/array.py:ArrowExtensionArray.dtype", "pandas/core/apply.py:FrameApply.values", "pandas/io/formats/info.py:_TableBuilderVerboseMixin.headers", "asv_bench/benchmarks/frame_methods.py:FindValidIndex.time_first_valid_index", "asv_bench/benchmarks/groupby.py:Datelike.time_sum", "asv_bench/benchmarks/frame_methods.py:Update.time_to_update_big_frame_small_arg", "pandas/core/arrays/arrow/array.py:ArrowExtensionArray._hasna", "asv_bench/benchmarks/frame_methods.py:Update.time_to_update_small_frame_big_arg", "asv_bench/benchmarks/groupby.py:SumMultiLevel.time_groupby_sum_multiindex", "pandas/core/arrays/masked.py:BaseMaskedArray._na_value", "pandas/core/dtypes/dtypes.py:DatetimeTZDtype.__str__", "asv_bench/benchmarks/groupby.py:SumTimeDe